# ArchesClimate-SSP

ArchesClimate-SSP (AC-SSP) is a deep-learning climate emulator based on [ArchesWeather](https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/sciadv.adx2372) trained to reproduce IPSL-CM6A-LR / CanESM5 monthly climate states under CMIP6 SSP scenarios. We condition AC-SSP on external forcings (GHGs, aerosols, ozone) given a scenario's forcing trajectory. With these forcings and two initial-condition months, AC-SSP generates autoregressively the rest of the scenario, orders of magnitude cheaper than
running the full Earth System Model (producing a subset of the variables).

This is the code for the paper
[**"Towards Emulating the Forced Response of Climate Models with Generative Machine Learning"**](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.16929).
It's a long-ago fork of [geoarches](https://github.com/INRIA/geoarches), the toolkit behind 
[ArchesWeather](https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/sciadv.adx2372).

![Annual-mean tas map, AC-SSP vs. IPSL-CM6A-LR, SSP4-3.4](assets/tas_rollout_map.gif)

*Annual-mean surface air temperature (tas), AC-SSP vs. the IPSL-CM6A-LR target it's conditioned to reproduce, SSP4-3.4*

![Global-mean tas timeseries, AC-SSP vs. IPSL-CM6A-LR, SSP4-3.4/SSP5-3.4-over/SSP5-8.5](assets/tas_rollout_timeseries.gif)

*Rollouts across three scenarios (SSP5-3.4 starts at 2040) as a lat-weighted global-mean timeseries (solid: AC-SSP, dashed: IPSL). Regenerate both GIFs with `python -m analysis.make_readme_gif`.*

## Quickstart

### 1. Install

Requires Python >=3.11 and [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/). From a clone of this repo:

```bash
uv sync
```

This installs the repo itself as an editable package (`ArchesClimate`, see `pyproject.toml`). The preprocessing pipeline has its own, separate `esmvaltool` environment (not part of this uv install, it was much easier to keep these separate), see `preprocessing/DOWNLOADING.md`.

### 2. Get model weights and data

Model weights and data are available on two Hugging Face repos: **[`gclyne/ArchesClimate`](https://huggingface.co/gclyne/ArchesClimate)**
(model weights) and **[`gclyne/ArchesClimate-data`](https://huggingface.co/datasets/gclyne/ArchesClimate-data)**
(everything physical: initial conditions, forcing trajectories, orography, normalization stats).
Three trained checkpoints are published: `archesclimate-ssp-deterministic` (IPSL-grid, single
free-running prediction per step), `archesclimate-ssp-energy-score` (IPSL-grid, energy-score-trained,
supports a stochastic ensemble), and `archesclimate-ssp-canesm5-energy-score` (CanESM5-native-grid,
energy-score-trained). To download the models and data, run these commands:

```bash
huggingface-cli download gclyne/ArchesClimate --local-dir <model_dir>
huggingface-cli download gclyne/ArchesClimate-data --local-dir <data_dir>
```

`<data_dir>` should end up holding `stats/cmip_stats_new_ozone.pt`, `static/orography.pt`
(+ `static/orography_canesm5_native.pt` for the CanESM5-grid checkpoint), and per-scenario
`scenarios/<realization>_<experiment>.pt` files (IC + full forcing trajectory together,
currently published for `ssp434`, `ssp534-over`, `ssp585`). `<model_dir>` holds each checkpoint's
`config.yaml` + `step-step=NNNNNN.ckpt`.

**The download alone is not enough to run a checkpoint.** The `cmip_stats_*.pt` file is loaded
at runtime as a package resource under `<repo>/stats/` — not from `<data_dir>`, wherever you
chose to put that. Copy or symlink it over:

```bash
ln -s "$(realpath <data_dir>/stats/cmip_stats_new_ozone.pt)" stats/cmip_stats_new_ozone.pt
```

See [`INFERENCE.md`](INFERENCE.md) §3 for why, and for every other required input.

Each published checkpoint has a matching `configs/module/<repo-subdir-name>.yaml` in this repo
(e.g. `configs/module/archesclimate-ssp-deterministic.yaml`), so `module=` can just reuse the
Hugging Face subdirectory name directly rather than needing the internal training-run config
name -- see [`INFERENCE.md`](INFERENCE.md) for the full rollout command.



Everything here is driven by [Hydra](https://hydra.cc/): a run is a composition of config
groups (`module`, `cluster`, `dataloader`, `inference`) under `configs/`, overridden on
the command line. 
See  [`TRAINING.md`](TRAINING.md) for how to train your own model. 
See [`INFERENCE.md`](INFERENCE.md) for how to run inference with a model. 
